            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Unity of Opposites</strong><br>\nCentral to Heraclitus's thinking was the idea that the universe is a unity of opposites. He posited that opposing forces give rise to each other and are interconnected in a harmonious balance. For instance, disease makes health sweet and good, just as hunger does for fullness and fatigue for rest.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Fire as the Primary Substance</strong><br>\nHeraclitus considered fire the primary substance that underlies all matter. He theorized that all things are an exchange for fire and fire for all things, just like goods for gold and gold for goods. This metaphor underlined his view of the transformative power of fire.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Critique of Previous Philosophers</strong><br>\nHeraclitus often criticized his philosophical predecessors and contemporaries for their failure to perceive the world's dynamic nature. He believed that understanding the world's constant change was essential to the pursuit of knowledge and wisdom.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
